00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.240
All right, let's get started everyone.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:35.820
So good afternoon.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.920
Welcome to the second lecture of 6.0001.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.380
And also of 600.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.580
So as always, if you'd like to follow along with the

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.600
lecture, with the lectures, please go ahead and

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:50.360
download the slides and the code that I'll provide at

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.420
least an hour before class every day.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.240
All right, so quick recap of what we did last time.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:59.180
So last time we talked a little bit about what a

00:00:59.180 --> 00:00:59.880
computer is.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:03.240
And I think the main takeaway from the last lecture is

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.760
really that a computer only does what it is told.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.700
So it's not going to spontaneously make

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:09.660
decisions on its own.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.260
You, as the programmer, have to tell it what you want it to

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:14.820
do by writing programs.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:20.220
OK, so we talked about simple objects, and these

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:21.900
objects were of different types.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.940
So we saw integers, floats, and booleans.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.960
And then we did a couple of simple operations with them.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:32.020
Today, we're going to look at a different, a new type of

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:33.000
object called a string.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.320
And then we're going to introduce some more powerful

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.840
things in our programming toolbox.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.760
So we're going to look at how to branch within a program and

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.900
how to make the computer repeat certain tasks

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:48.940
within our program.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:53.140
All right, so let's begin by looking at strings.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.080
So strings are a new object type.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:58.260
We've seen so far integers, which were whole

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.560
numbers, floats, which were decimal numbers.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.560
And we've seen booleans which were true and false.

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.960
So strings are going to be sequences of characters.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.140
And these characters can be anything.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:14.820
They could be letters, digits, special characters, and

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:15.480
also spaces.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.060
And you tell Python that you're talking about a string

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.080
object by enclosing it in quotation marks.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:28.780
So in this case, I'm creating an object whose value is

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.700
H-E-L-L-O space, T-H-E-R-E.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:35.160
And Python knows it's a string object because we're enclosing

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:35.920
it in quotations.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.520
They could be either double quotes or single quotes, but

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.160
as long as you're consistent, it doesn't matter.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:45.240
And this object, we're binding it to this variable

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:45.920
named high.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:49.360
And we're using that using the equal sign, which is the

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:50.080
assignment operator.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.900
OK, so from now on, whenever we refer to this

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:56.940
variable high, Python's going to say, oh, I know what

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.480
the value is, and it's that string of characters.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:03.280
So we're going to learn about two things that you can do on

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:04.720
strings today, two operations.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:06.560
One is to concatenate them.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.460
And concatenation is really just a fancy word for using

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.640
this plus operator, which means put the strings

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:13.020
together.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:18.320
So I have this original variable named high.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:20.160
And I create a new variable called name.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.560
And in it, I'm going to assign the string

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.260
ANA to the variable name.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.860
And when I use the plus operator in between high and name,

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.620
those two variables, Python's going to look at the values

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:36.480
of those two, and it's going to just put them together.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.660
I'm going to switch to spider.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:49.020
And this is just that example from the slides.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:50.540
So let's see what happens.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.320
So I have the variable high.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:54.140
The variable name.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.400
And I'm just concatenating those two together.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:58.560
And then I'm going to print that out.

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:04.000
So if I run the code, notice it prints out hello there, Anna.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:05.980
There's no space.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.140
And there's no space because the concatenation operator,

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:13.360
the plus, doesn't add any spaces implicitly.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:17.040
So again, another example of just doing what it's told.

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:20.400
If we want to add a space, we'd have to actually insert

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:21.360
the space manually.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.060
So that's this line here, line eight.

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:28.440
And in this line, we're concatenating the value

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.860
of the variable high with a space.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:34.380
Notice we're putting it in quotation marks, just a space.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:35.480
And then with name.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.420
So if we go ahead and print that value,

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:45.160
notice this was that garbage greeting there.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:46.700
And now we have a proper greeting.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:55.850
So that's the concatenation between strings.

00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:59.710
And then the other thing we're going to look at related

00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:01.970
to strings is the star operator.

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:06.190
So that's this one here on line 10.

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:09.830
So Python allows you to use the star operator, which is

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:13.970
stands for multiplication, between a string and a number.

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:18.950
And when you do that, Python interprets it

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:23.830
as repeat that string that many number of times.

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:28.030
So in this case, I'm creating a silly greeting.

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:32.690
And I'm concatenating the value of high, which is hello there,

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:36.170
with the space plus the name.

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:39.410
So notice here I'm using parentheses to tell Python

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:40.750
do this operation first.

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:45.630
And then multiply whatever the result of this is by three.

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:53.130
So if I print that out, it's going to multiply the space

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:54.850
with my name three times.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:57.630
And it's going to concatenate that with hello there.

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:00.690
So that's exactly what it printed out there.

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:04.910
Last lecture, we talked a little bit about print.

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:08.970
Today I'm going to talk about some nuances related to print.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:10.870
So you use print to interact with the user.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:15.010
It's cool to write programs that print things out to the user.

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:17.990
So the keyword here being print.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:21.750
And then you put parentheses after print.

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:25.690
And in the parentheses, you put in whatever you want to show

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:26.050
the user.

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:31.590
So in this little program, I create a variable named x.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:33.130
I assign it the value one.

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:34.050
And then I print one.

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:37.170
Here I'm casting.

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:40.710
So I'm taking the number one, the integer one,

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:42.110
and I'm casting it to a string.

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:44.550
And you'll see why in a moment.

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:48.430
So I want to bring to your attention a couple things here.

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:53.470
So in the first print, I'm using commas everywhere here.

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:56.270
And in the second print, I'm using plus.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:06.730
So by definition, you can use commas inside the parentheses

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:07.330
of print.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:13.470
And if you use a comma, Python's going to automatically

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:16.330
add a space in between the two things

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:19.410
that the comma is in between, the values.

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:22.890
So my fav num is the first thing.

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:25.770
And the second thing is whatever is after the comma.

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:27.910
Let's take x.

00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:31.530
So if you use a comma, Python's going to automatically

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:32.810
insert a space for you.

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:35.530
Sometimes you might want that.

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:36.430
Sometimes you might not.

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:39.190
If you don't want that, you can use the concatenation

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:40.970
operation, the plus.

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:44.690
And you can add all of your little bits together

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:45.970
to create one big string.

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:52.230
If you're using commas, the objects in between the commas

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:53.630
do not all have to be strings.

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:55.930
That's the plus side of using commas.

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:58.850
But the downside is you get spaces everywhere.

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:03.530
If you use plus operator, the plus side

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:06.470
is Python does exactly what you tell it to do.

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:09.110
But everything has to be a string object.

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:12.090
So my fav num is a string object.

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:15.130
You have to convert all of your numbers to string objects

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:16.270
and so on.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:30.340
So if we look at spider, this is almost the same code.

00:08:30.940 --> 00:08:32.860
So here I don't have spaces anywhere.

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:36.720
So you can see that the first line here

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:38.720
has commas everywhere.

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:42.980
So I'm going to have spaces in between every one of the things

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:45.240
that I'm printing out.

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:48.780
This line here is sort of a combination

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:52.500
between commas and concatenation.

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:56.460
So depending on where I use the comma,

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:57.740
I'm going to have an extra space.

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:00.960
And this line here just has concatenation everywhere.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:06.600
So if I run this, notice this very first line

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:09.760
added spaces everywhere in between all my objects.

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:11.520
The second one added spaces somewhere.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:14.160
And you can sort of trace through and see exactly where

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:15.120
the spaces were added.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:20.360
And the last line here didn't add spaces anywhere.

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:36.980
So printing things out to the console is nice.

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:40.680
But the second part of writing an interactive program

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:42.220
is getting input from the user.

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:45.060
And that's a more interesting part.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:46.860
So if you've done problem set zero,

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:49.220
you might have sort of already tried

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:50.420
to understand this on your own.

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:51.120
But here we are.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:55.480
So the way you get input from the user

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:58.460
is using this command function called input.

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:01.220
And inside the parentheses,

00:10:02.020 --> 00:10:06.300
you type in whatever you'd like to prompt the user with.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:09.680
So in this case, in my example here,

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:12.760
I have input and then here I said type anything.

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:15.980
So the user is going to see this text here.

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:19.040
And then the program is just going to stop.

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:21.540
And it's going to wait for the user to type in something

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:22.360
and hit Enter.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:25.300
As soon as the user types in Enter,

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.840
whatever the user types in becomes a string.

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:33.260
If the user types in a number, for example,

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:36.260
that becomes the string, that number.

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:38.040
So everything the user types in

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:39.780
is going to be made as a string.

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:44.340
In this line right here,

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:47.020
whatever the user types in becomes a string.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:50.400
And we're going to bind that string object

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:52.880
to this variable named called text.

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:55.820
So now further in my program,

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:57.900
I could do whatever I want with this variable text.

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:01.060
In this case, I'm going to print five star text.

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:06.360
So if the user, for example, gave me ha,

00:11:06.960 --> 00:11:08.820
I'm going to print ha five times.

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:11.940
If the user gave me five,

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:14.600
what do you think the user's,

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:15.860
what do you think's going to be printed out?

00:11:18.430 --> 00:11:20.590
25 or five, five times?

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:24.510
Great, yes, exactly, five, five times.

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:31.200
Oftentimes, you don't want to work with numbers as strings.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.560
You want to work with numbers as numbers.

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:35.980
So you have to cast.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:37.860
And we learned that last lecture.

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:40.780
You cast by just putting in this little bit

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:41.920
right in front of the input.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:45.040
And you can cast it to whatever type you want.

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:46.460
Here, I cast it to an int,

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:47.960
but you can also cast to a float.

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:49.200
If you want to work with floats.

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:53.700
And that converts whatever the user typed in,

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:55.740
as long as it's some number that Python knows

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:58.140
how to convert into the number itself.

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:00.900
So in this case, if the user gives me five,

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:03.500
I'm going to print out five times five,

00:12:03.660 --> 00:12:05.600
instead of five, five times.

00:12:07.880 --> 00:12:12.130
So that's the code here.

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:16.800
So the first bit is I'm going to get the user

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:17.680
to type in anything,

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:22.980
and I'm going to put five, five, five.

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:24.240
And then when I type in the number,

00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:25.580
since I'm casting it,

00:12:25.660 --> 00:12:27.000
I'm going to do operations with the number.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:28.380
Yeah, question.

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:33.400
Why do you want to cast to?

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:39.300
Oh, the question is why do you want to cast to a string?

00:12:40.720 --> 00:12:42.680
Why do you want to cast a string to a number?

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:51.820
Oh, so Python always, whatever you type in,

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:54.840
just by default, by definition of the input command,

00:12:55.180 --> 00:12:56.520
Python always makes it a string.

00:12:57.880 --> 00:12:59.340
So if you want to work with numbers,

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:00.700
you have to explicitly tell it,

00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:01.740
I'm going to work with a number.

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:04.560
So even if you give it the number five,

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:06.160
it's going to think it's the string five.

00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:10.340
Yeah, that's just how input works.

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:17.480
The next thing we're going to look at

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:24.220
is ways that you can start adding tests in your code.

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:27.300
And before you can start adding tests,

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:29.600
before you can start adding tests in your code,

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:32.360
you need to be able to do the actual tests.

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:37.360
So this is where comparison operators come in.

00:13:39.440 --> 00:13:42.080
So here, let's assume that INJ are variables.

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:47.760
The following comparisons are going to give you a boolean.

00:13:48.020 --> 00:13:50.420
So it's either going to say this is true or this is false.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:52.580
So that's going to be your test.

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:56.150
So if INJ are variables,

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.830
you're allowed to compare ints with ints,

00:13:58.930 --> 00:14:00.910
floats with floats, strings with strings,

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:03.790
and you're allowed to compare ints and floats

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:05.610
between themselves, but you're not allowed

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:07.770
to compare a string with a number.

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:11.930
In fact, if you even try to do that in Python,

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:14.410
in spite here, if I try to say,

00:14:14.530 --> 00:14:16.350
is the letter A greater than five?

00:14:17.930 --> 00:14:21.130
I get some angry text right here.

00:14:22.270 --> 00:14:23.310
And this just tells me,

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:25.210
Python doesn't understand the meaning

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:27.790
of how do I compare a string with a number, right?

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:35.900
So just like in math, we can do these usual comparisons.

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:38.040
We can say if something's greater than something,

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:40.060
greater or equal to, less than less than or equal to.

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:42.860
I'd like to bring to your attention the equality.

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:46.700
So the single equal sign is an assignment.

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:49.160
So you're taking a value and you're signing it to a variable,

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:51.300
but when you're doing the double equal sign,

00:14:51.400 --> 00:14:52.760
this is the test for equality.

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:55.740
Is the value of variable i the same

00:14:55.740 --> 00:14:57.220
as the value of the variable j?

00:14:57.220 --> 00:15:00.320
And that's, again, also going to give you a Boolean,

00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:01.400
either true or false.

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:03.580
And you can also test for inequality

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:05.660
with the exclamation equal.

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:09.160
So that means, is the value of the variable i

00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:11.240
not equal to the value of the variable j?

00:15:11.900 --> 00:15:13.800
True if, yes, false if, no.

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:18.560
So those are comparison operators

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:20.300
on integer, floats, and strings.

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:23.640
On Booleans, you can do some logic operators.

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:29.520
And the simplest is just inverting.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:35.380
So if a is a variable that has a Boolean value,

00:15:35.820 --> 00:15:37.160
not a is just going to invert it.

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:40.320
So if a is true, then not a is false and vice versa.

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:45.820
This is a table that sort of represents what I've said here

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:49.440
so you can do, you can use and and or.

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:50.680
These are keywords in Python.

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:55.460
You can use those two keywords on Boolean variables.

00:15:57.740 --> 00:16:01.780
And you get the result a and b is only true

00:16:01.780 --> 00:16:03.300
if both a and b are true.

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:10.240
And a or b is only false if a and b are false.

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:12.260
And this is the complete table,

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:14.280
just in case you need to reference it.

00:16:17.460 --> 00:16:18.900
All right, so now that we have ways

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:21.240
to do logical test, yep, question right there.

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:27.280
Yeah, great question.

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:28.800
So what does it mean to compare a string

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:30.200
with a string with a greater than?

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:33.440
So that's just going to compare them lexicographically.

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:35.420
So does it come first in the alphabet?

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:38.540
So we can even test that out.

00:16:39.300 --> 00:16:44.940
We can say is a greater than b and it's false.

00:16:47.950 --> 00:16:49.750
So b becomes later in the alphabet than a.

00:16:53.750 --> 00:16:55.850
Okay, so now we have ways to do the tests.

00:16:56.510 --> 00:17:01.170
So we can add some branching

00:17:01.170 --> 00:17:02.710
to our programming toolbox now

00:17:02.710 --> 00:17:04.130
that we have ways to do tests.

00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:05.950
This is a map of MIT.

00:17:06.710 --> 00:17:10.150
I'm going to go through sort of a little example

00:17:10.150 --> 00:17:14.830
to motivate why we want to do branching in our code.

00:17:15.430 --> 00:17:16.390
And I think after this lecture

00:17:16.390 --> 00:17:18.270
you'll be able to sort of code up this algorithm

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:19.290
that I'm going to explain.

00:17:20.110 --> 00:17:21.690
So most of us see MIT as a maze.

00:17:21.910 --> 00:17:23.330
I first did when I came here.

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:27.950
When I first came here obviously

00:17:27.950 --> 00:17:29.810
I signed up for the free food mailing list.

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:34.150
And MIT being a maze I had no idea where to go, right?

00:17:34.310 --> 00:17:35.890
What the shortest path was to free food.

00:17:38.050 --> 00:17:40.930
So one way to think about it is all I wanted to do

00:17:40.930 --> 00:17:41.910
was get to the free food.

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:46.390
A very simple algorithm to get there

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:49.170
would be to say, okay, I'm going to take my right hand

00:17:49.170 --> 00:17:50.810
and I'm going to make sure that my right hand

00:17:50.810 --> 00:17:52.870
is always on a wall, okay?

00:17:53.150 --> 00:17:54.410
And I'm going to go around campus

00:17:54.410 --> 00:17:56.290
with my right hand always being at a wall

00:17:56.770 --> 00:17:59.010
and eventually I'll get to where the free food is.

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:00.650
There might not be any left, right?

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:02.330
But I'll be there, okay.

00:18:03.010 --> 00:18:04.950
So the algorithm is as follows, okay?

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:07.250
If my right hand always has to be on a wall

00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:11.610
then I'm going to say if there's no wall to my right side

00:18:11.610 --> 00:18:15.270
then I'm going to go right until I get to a wall, okay?

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:21.580
Then if there's a wall to my right

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:23.740
and I can go forward I'm just going to keep going forward,

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:24.660
okay?

00:18:25.900 --> 00:18:28.340
If I keep going forward and there's a wall to my right

00:18:28.340 --> 00:18:29.660
and in front of me I'm going to turn around

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:31.040
and go left, okay?

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:34.400
And then if there's a wall to my right in front of me

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:36.200
and to the left then I'm going to turn around and go back.

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:39.240
So with this fairly simple algorithm

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:44.780
I just follow the path always keeping the wall to my right

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:49.340
and eventually I end up where I need to be, okay?

00:18:49.960 --> 00:18:53.900
So notice I used just in plain English a few keywords, okay?

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:56.780
If, otherwise, things like that.

00:18:57.460 --> 00:19:00.800
So in programming we have those same constructs

00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:03.680
and those same sort of intuitive words

00:19:03.680 --> 00:19:06.660
can be used to tell Python to do something

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:08.160
or to do something else

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:12.740
or to choose from a different set of possibilities, okay?

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:16.260
And this way we can get the computer

00:19:16.260 --> 00:19:17.560
to make decisions for us.

00:19:18.380 --> 00:19:19.860
And you might be thinking, well,

00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:22.240
you said that computers can't make decisions on their own.

00:19:22.820 --> 00:19:23.500
It's not.

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:26.460
US programmers are going to build these decisions

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:29.020
into the program and all the computer's going to do

00:19:29.020 --> 00:19:31.320
is going to reach the decision point and say,

00:19:31.720 --> 00:19:33.420
okay, this is a decision point.

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:34.740
Should I go left or should I go right?

00:19:35.120 --> 00:19:35.980
Or which one do I pick?

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:40.000
And these sort of decisions are created by you as a programmer

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:42.180
and the computer just has to make the decision

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:44.180
and choose a path, okay?

00:19:44.880 --> 00:19:47.580
So in programming there's three sort of simple ways

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:50.600
that you can add control flow to your programs

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:52.260
and that's making one decision

00:19:52.750 --> 00:19:54.660
and choosing whether to execute something

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:56.340
or execute something else.

00:19:57.460 --> 00:20:00.480
The first is the simple if, okay?

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:05.140
And given a program that just linearly has statements

00:20:05.140 --> 00:20:06.100
that get executed,

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:09.640
whenever I reach an if statement,

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:12.960
you're going to check the condition.

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:15.260
The condition's going to be something

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:18.580
that's going to get evaluated to either true or false.

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:23.140
Okay, so I've reached the condition here.

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:26.400
And if the condition's true,

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:28.460
then I'm going to additionally execute

00:20:28.460 --> 00:20:30.080
this extra set of expressions,

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:32.680
but if the condition's false,

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:35.300
then I'm just gonna keep going through the program

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:38.640
and not execute that extra set of instructions, okay?

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:43.500
How does Python know which instructions to execute?

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:45.740
They're going to be inside this,

00:20:45.820 --> 00:20:46.960
what we call code block

00:20:48.040 --> 00:20:51.060
and the code blocks denoted by indentation.

00:20:51.360 --> 00:20:53.740
So it's going to be everything that's indented

00:20:53.740 --> 00:20:56.360
is part of that if code block.

00:20:57.820 --> 00:20:59.700
Typically four spaces indentation.

00:21:01.140 --> 00:21:06.200
Okay, so that's how you'd write code

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:09.420
that decides whether to execute this extra thing or not.

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:13.540
Now let's say I don't just want to execute

00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:15.400
an extra thing, I want to reach a condition,

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:17.100
I want to reach a point where I say,

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:18.680
I'll either go down this path

00:21:19.310 --> 00:21:21.740
or I'll do something else, okay?

00:21:22.500 --> 00:21:26.180
That's this right here.

00:21:27.520 --> 00:21:31.620
So this if else construct says,

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:35.720
this is my code, I've reached my decision point here.

00:21:36.660 --> 00:21:40.440
If the condition inside this if is true,

00:21:41.580 --> 00:21:44.900
then I'm going to execute maybe this set of statements here.

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:50.260
But if the condition's not true,

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:53.640
then I'm not going to execute that set of statements

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:55.140
and instead I'm going to execute

00:21:55.140 --> 00:21:58.440
under whatever else is.

00:22:00.340 --> 00:22:01.800
So using this construct,

00:22:01.960 --> 00:22:04.020
I'm either going to do one set of expressions

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:06.400
or the other, but never both, okay?

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:08.460
And after I've executed one or the other,

00:22:08.580 --> 00:22:09.660
I'm going to continue on

00:22:09.660 --> 00:22:11.780
with just the regular execution of the program.

00:22:17.010 --> 00:22:17.370
All right?

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:22.140
Okay, so we're able to either choose one thing,

00:22:22.240 --> 00:22:23.040
choose one thing or another,

00:22:23.160 --> 00:22:26.740
but what if we want to have more than one choice?

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:30.840
So if some number is equal to zero,

00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:31.540
I want to do this.

00:22:31.640 --> 00:22:33.000
If it's equal to one, I want to do this.

00:22:33.080 --> 00:22:34.320
Otherwise, if it's equal to two,

00:22:34.360 --> 00:22:35.400
I want to do this and so on.

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:37.820
That's where this last one comes in.

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:43.480
And we introduce this other keyword here called L if.

00:22:45.400 --> 00:22:47.840
So that stands for short form for else if.

00:22:49.020 --> 00:22:52.060
So first we check if this condition is true.

00:22:52.780 --> 00:22:54.200
So we're going through our program.

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:56.000
We've reached our decision point.

00:22:56.800 --> 00:22:58.160
If the condition's true,

00:22:58.160 --> 00:23:00.800
we're going to execute maybe this set of instructions.

00:23:03.940 --> 00:23:06.660
If the condition's not true, maybe we'll check.

00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:09.380
If the condition's not true,

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:11.560
we will check this next condition.

00:23:11.960 --> 00:23:14.080
That's part of the L if right here.

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:15.880
And if that one's true,

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:17.760
we're going to execute a different set of instructions.

00:23:18.500 --> 00:23:20.480
You can have more than one L if.

00:23:21.060 --> 00:23:22.620
And depending on which one's true,

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:24.600
you're going to execute a different set of instructions.

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:28.300
And then this last else is sort of a catch all

00:23:28.300 --> 00:23:31.260
where if none of the previous conditions were true,

00:23:31.380 --> 00:23:34.200
then just do this last set of expressions.

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:36.780
So in this case, you're going to choose

00:23:37.320 --> 00:23:38.840
between one of these three,

00:23:39.220 --> 00:23:41.820
one of these four roots or however many you have.

00:23:43.160 --> 00:23:44.920
And then when you're done making your choice,

00:23:44.980 --> 00:23:47.140
you're going to execute the remaining set of instructions.

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:53.660
So the way this works is if more than one condition

00:23:53.660 --> 00:23:57.340
is true, you're actually just going to enter one of them

00:23:57.760 --> 00:23:59.600
and you're going to enter the very first one

00:23:59.600 --> 00:24:00.180
that's true.

00:24:00.840 --> 00:24:02.120
So you're never going to enter

00:24:02.120 --> 00:24:05.080
more than one of these code blocks.

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:08.120
You always enter one and you enter the first one

00:24:08.120 --> 00:24:09.840
that evaluates to true.

00:24:15.700 --> 00:24:18.940
So notice that we denoted code blocks using indentation.

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:21.020
And that's actually one of the things

00:24:21.020 --> 00:24:22.220
that I really like about Python.

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:25.000
It sort of forces you to write pretty code

00:24:25.540 --> 00:24:29.580
and nice looking code and just code that's very readable.

00:24:31.460 --> 00:24:33.340
And that enforces you to indent

00:24:33.340 --> 00:24:34.840
everything that's a code block.

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:38.940
So you can easily see sort of where the flow of control is

00:24:38.940 --> 00:24:40.520
and where decision making points are

00:24:41.220 --> 00:24:42.320
and things like that.

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:46.460
So in this particular example,

00:24:46.600 --> 00:24:48.540
we have one if statement here

00:24:49.280 --> 00:24:51.480
and it checks if two variables are equal

00:24:55.060 --> 00:24:57.060
and we have an if, L, if, else.

00:24:58.400 --> 00:25:00.320
And in this example, we're going to enter

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:03.620
either this code block or this one or this one

00:25:03.620 --> 00:25:05.500
depending on the variables of X and Y

00:25:05.860 --> 00:25:07.940
and we're only going to enter one code block

00:25:08.420 --> 00:25:11.060
and we'll enter the first one that's true.

00:25:13.340 --> 00:25:15.880
Notice you can have nested conditionals.

00:25:16.040 --> 00:25:18.880
So inside this first if, we have another if here

00:25:22.850 --> 00:25:26.590
and this inner if is only going to be checked

00:25:27.450 --> 00:25:30.350
when we enter the first, this outer if.

00:25:31.710 --> 00:25:37.820
I do want to make one point though.

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:41.740
So sometimes you might forget to do the double equal side

00:25:41.740 --> 00:25:44.200
when you check, when you're checking for equality

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:44.840
and that's okay.

00:25:45.880 --> 00:25:47.620
If you just use one equal sign,

00:25:47.720 --> 00:25:49.640
Python's going to give you an error

00:25:50.760 --> 00:25:53.000
and it's going to say syntax error

00:25:53.480 --> 00:25:55.020
and it's going to highlight this line

00:25:55.600 --> 00:25:56.660
and then you're going to know that

00:25:56.660 --> 00:25:58.640
there's a mistake there.

00:25:58.760 --> 00:25:59.660
You should be using equality

00:26:00.220 --> 00:26:04.320
because it doesn't make sense to be making an assignment

00:26:04.320 --> 00:26:05.240
inside the if.

00:26:06.900 --> 00:26:11.090
Okay, all right.

00:26:12.230 --> 00:26:13.790
So we've learned about branching

00:26:13.790 --> 00:26:14.890
and we know about conditionals.

00:26:16.930 --> 00:26:21.150
Let's try to apply this to a little game

00:26:22.890 --> 00:26:24.710
and spoiler, we won't be able to.

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:26.150
We'll have to learn about a new thing.

00:26:26.850 --> 00:26:29.570
But back in 1980s, there was the Legend of Zelda

00:26:29.570 --> 00:26:33.770
cool graphics where there was a scene with

00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:37.270
the Lost Woods over simplification

00:26:37.270 --> 00:26:40.270
if anyone's a Zelda die hard fan

00:26:40.750 --> 00:26:44.070
but the basic idea was if you entered the woods

00:26:44.590 --> 00:26:46.570
you entered from the left to the right

00:26:47.110 --> 00:26:49.710
and then as long as you kept going right

00:26:49.710 --> 00:26:51.630
it would show you the same screen over and over again.

00:26:53.050 --> 00:26:55.510
And the trick was you just had to go backward

00:26:55.510 --> 00:26:57.670
and then you'd exit the woods.

00:26:58.190 --> 00:26:59.070
So very simple.

00:27:00.050 --> 00:27:03.050
Using what we know so far we could sort of code this up

00:27:04.230 --> 00:27:05.910
and we'd say something like this.

00:27:06.070 --> 00:27:07.470
If the user exits right

00:27:07.470 --> 00:27:09.870
then set the background to the woods background.

00:27:11.430 --> 00:27:13.730
Otherwise set the background to the exit background.

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:18.290
Now let's say the user and then in the else were done.

00:27:18.850 --> 00:27:20.350
Let's say the user went right

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:22.650
while you'd show them the woods background

00:27:22.650 --> 00:27:24.730
and now ask them again where do they want to go

00:27:25.150 --> 00:27:26.050
if they exit right,

00:27:26.310 --> 00:27:27.730
set the background to the woods background

00:27:27.730 --> 00:27:29.670
otherwise set the background to the exit background.

00:27:30.470 --> 00:27:30.890
And so on.

00:27:30.950 --> 00:27:34.470
So you notice that there's sort of no end to this, right?

00:27:35.050 --> 00:27:36.430
How many times do you know?

00:27:36.970 --> 00:27:39.270
Do you know how many times the user might keep going right?

00:27:39.390 --> 00:27:40.770
They might be really persistent right?

00:27:41.290 --> 00:27:43.990
And they'll be like maybe if I go a thousand times

00:27:43.990 --> 00:27:44.930
I'll get out of the woods.

00:27:45.590 --> 00:27:46.510
Or maybe a thousand and one.

00:27:47.290 --> 00:27:47.770
Maybe.

00:27:48.650 --> 00:27:54.250
So this would probably be who knows how deep

00:27:55.730 --> 00:27:57.050
these nested ifs.

00:27:57.390 --> 00:27:58.110
So we don't know.

00:28:00.730 --> 00:28:01.970
So with what we know so far

00:28:01.970 --> 00:28:04.110
we can't really code this cute little game.

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:06.730
But enter loops.

00:28:07.630 --> 00:28:09.290
And specifically a while loop.

00:28:10.910 --> 00:28:14.490
So this code here that could be infinitely

00:28:14.960 --> 00:28:17.250
number of nested if statements deep

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:19.670
can be rewritten using these three lines.

00:28:21.250 --> 00:28:24.230
So we say while the user exits right

00:28:24.230 --> 00:28:25.830
set the background to the woods background

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:27.810
and with a while loop

00:28:27.810 --> 00:28:29.990
it's going to do what we tell it to do inside the loop

00:28:29.990 --> 00:28:31.610
and then it's going to check the condition again

00:28:31.610 --> 00:28:34.490
and then it's going to do what we say

00:28:34.490 --> 00:28:36.710
it should do inside the code block

00:28:36.710 --> 00:28:38.870
and then it's going to check the condition again.

00:28:39.690 --> 00:28:40.590
And then when the condition,

00:28:41.050 --> 00:28:42.450
as long as the condition is true

00:28:42.450 --> 00:28:44.670
it's going to keep doing that little loop there.

00:28:45.250 --> 00:28:46.970
And as soon as the condition becomes false

00:28:46.970 --> 00:28:48.210
it's going to stop doing the loop

00:28:48.210 --> 00:28:50.330
and do whatever is right after the while.

00:28:52.870 --> 00:28:56.690
So that's basically how a while loop works.

00:28:57.290 --> 00:28:59.090
We have while, that's the keyword.

00:29:00.030 --> 00:29:02.050
The condition is something that gets evaluated

00:29:02.050 --> 00:29:02.990
to true or false

00:29:03.470 --> 00:29:06.170
and once again we have a code block that's indented

00:29:06.690 --> 00:29:09.330
and it tells Python these are the expressions I want to do

00:29:09.330 --> 00:29:11.030
as long as the condition is true.

00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:17.340
So the condition is true

00:29:17.340 --> 00:29:19.340
you evaluate every expression in the code block

00:29:19.340 --> 00:29:20.920
when you reach the end of the expression

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:23.420
end of the code block you check the condition again

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:26.620
if it's true still you keep doing the expressions

00:29:27.100 --> 00:29:28.720
check it again and so on.

00:29:32.620 --> 00:29:33.840
So here's a little game

00:29:35.840 --> 00:29:38.240
and with these lines of code we were able

00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:42.200
we can code up the last words of Zelda.

00:29:43.020 --> 00:29:44.940
