00:31:28.900 --> 00:31:33.040
Okay so so far we've used while loops

00:31:33.040 --> 00:31:34.380
to ask for user input

00:31:34.380 --> 00:31:37.280
and that's actually somewhere where it makes sense

00:31:37.280 --> 00:31:39.260
to use while loops because you don't actually know

00:31:39.260 --> 00:31:41.220
how many times the user's going to type in something.

00:31:42.920 --> 00:31:46.620
You can use while loops to keep sort of a counter

00:31:46.620 --> 00:31:49.500
and to write code that counts something.

00:31:52.080 --> 00:31:54.940
If you do that though there's two things

00:31:54.940 --> 00:31:56.060
you need to take care of.

00:31:56.280 --> 00:31:58.200
The first is this first line here

00:31:58.200 --> 00:32:04.050
which is sort of an initialization of this loop counter

00:32:06.610 --> 00:32:08.410
and the second is this line here

00:32:09.130 --> 00:32:11.890
which is incrementing your loop counter.

00:32:15.380 --> 00:32:17.680
The reason why the second one's important

00:32:17.680 --> 00:32:20.160
is because let's look at our condition here.

00:32:20.700 --> 00:32:22.180
So while n is less than five.

00:32:23.740 --> 00:32:25.740
If you didn't have this line here

00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:28.160
you would never increment n.

00:32:28.760 --> 00:32:30.960
So every time through the loop you'd just keep

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:34.100
printing zeros okay and you'd have an infinite loop.

00:32:34.820 --> 00:32:37.680
I do want to show though what if you do have

00:32:37.680 --> 00:32:39.500
an infinite loop it's not the end of the world.

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:52.080
So I can say something like so while true print zero.

00:32:52.620 --> 00:32:54.860
So this is going to give me an infinite loop

00:32:54.860 --> 00:33:07.030
in my program and whoop, okay.

00:33:08.150 --> 00:33:10.910
So notice it's just printing the letter p

00:33:10.910 --> 00:33:13.670
over and over again and if I let it go any longer

00:33:13.670 --> 00:33:14.650
it's gonna slow down the computer.

00:33:14.990 --> 00:33:17.450
So I'm gonna hit control C or command C maybe

00:33:18.390 --> 00:33:22.150
and it's going to stop the program from printing okay.

00:33:22.510 --> 00:33:24.590
So just in case you ever enter infinite loops

00:33:24.590 --> 00:33:26.950
in your programs just go to the console

00:33:26.950 --> 00:33:29.490
and hit control C and that's going to stop it

00:33:29.490 --> 00:33:32.610
from sort of slowing down the computer.

00:33:34.470 --> 00:33:36.330
Okay so going back to this example

00:33:36.330 --> 00:33:37.890
I was saying that if you're using

00:33:38.370 --> 00:33:41.450
counters or variables in order to sort of count up

00:33:41.450 --> 00:33:43.250
inside the while loop you have to take care

00:33:43.250 --> 00:33:45.850
to initialize a counter variable first

00:33:46.550 --> 00:33:48.450
and then to increment it.

00:33:48.550 --> 00:33:49.970
Otherwise you'll enter an infinite loop.

00:33:50.990 --> 00:33:52.450
That feels a little bit tedious

00:33:53.310 --> 00:33:57.570
and so there's a shortcut for doing that exact same thing.

00:33:57.690 --> 00:34:00.130
So these four lines you can rewrite those

00:34:00.130 --> 00:34:01.790
into these two lines right here

00:34:02.310 --> 00:34:04.990
using this new type of loop called a for loop.

00:34:07.120 --> 00:34:10.100
So the for loop says for some loop variable

00:34:10.100 --> 00:34:13.040
in this case I named it n you can name it whatever you want.

00:34:13.720 --> 00:34:15.440
In range five we're going to come back

00:34:15.440 --> 00:34:18.260
to what range means in a little bit print n.

00:34:21.680 --> 00:34:24.180
So every time through the loop you're going to print out

00:34:24.180 --> 00:34:25.480
what the value of n is.

00:34:26.600 --> 00:34:30.980
Range five actually creates internally

00:34:30.980 --> 00:34:33.720
a sequence of numbers starting from zero

00:34:33.720 --> 00:34:36.220
and going to that number five minus one.

00:34:36.740 --> 00:34:40.060
So the sequence is going to be zero, one, two, three and four.

00:34:41.580 --> 00:34:43.860
The first time through the loop you're going to say

00:34:43.860 --> 00:34:46.920
n is equal to zero or internally this is what happens

00:34:46.920 --> 00:34:49.560
n gets the value zero you're going to print n.

00:34:50.740 --> 00:34:53.440
Then you're going to go back to the top

00:34:53.440 --> 00:34:55.920
n gets the value one then you're going to go

00:34:55.920 --> 00:34:58.920
execute whatever's inside so you're going to print one.

00:35:00.360 --> 00:35:01.620
Then you're going to increment that

00:35:01.620 --> 00:35:03.040
to the next value in the sequence

00:35:03.040 --> 00:35:05.260
you're going to print out two and so on.

00:35:07.480 --> 00:35:11.840
So this is the general look of a for loop.

00:35:12.040 --> 00:35:15.800
So we have for some loop variable again

00:35:15.800 --> 00:35:19.840
can be named whatever you want in range some number

00:35:21.020 --> 00:35:24.760
do a bunch of stuff and again

00:35:24.760 --> 00:35:26.720
these are part of this for loop code block

00:35:26.720 --> 00:35:28.980
so you should indent them to tell Python

00:35:28.980 --> 00:35:30.580
that these are the things that you should do.

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:34.220
So when you're using range some number

00:35:34.220 --> 00:35:38.840
you start out with variable getting the value zero.

00:35:41.780 --> 00:35:44.120
With variable having value zero

00:35:44.120 --> 00:35:46.060
you're going to execute all of these expressions.

00:35:47.580 --> 00:35:50.280
After all the expressions in the code block are done

00:35:50.280 --> 00:35:54.460
you're going to go on to the next value so one.

00:35:55.480 --> 00:35:57.620
You're going to execute all these expressions

00:35:57.620 --> 00:36:02.460
with variable being value one and then so on and so on

00:36:02.460 --> 00:36:05.260
until you go to sum num minus one.

00:36:11.180 --> 00:36:14.680
So using range in that way is a little bit constraining

00:36:15.400 --> 00:36:17.980
because you're always going to get values starting from zero

00:36:17.980 --> 00:36:20.640
and ending at sum num minus one.

00:36:21.240 --> 00:36:23.160
Whatever's in the parentheses in range.

00:36:23.980 --> 00:36:25.380
Sometimes you might want to write programs

00:36:25.380 --> 00:36:27.200
that maybe start at a custom value.

00:36:27.480 --> 00:36:29.100
Don't start at zero maybe they start at five

00:36:29.680 --> 00:36:31.020
maybe it started minus ten.

00:36:32.360 --> 00:36:34.020
And sometimes you might want to write programs

00:36:34.020 --> 00:36:36.300
that don't go with don't increment

00:36:36.300 --> 00:36:39.000
the numbers by one but maybe skip every other number

00:36:39.000 --> 00:36:41.420
or go every two numbers every three numbers and so on.

00:36:41.940 --> 00:36:45.680
So you can customize range to your needs.

00:36:47.020 --> 00:36:49.440
The one thing you do need to give it is the stop.

00:36:50.040 --> 00:36:52.120
So if you give it only one value in the parentheses

00:36:52.120 --> 00:36:53.800
that stands for stop.

00:36:54.700 --> 00:36:57.360
And by default start is going to have the value zero

00:36:57.360 --> 00:36:59.260
and step is going to have the value one.

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:03.420
If you give it two things in the parentheses

00:37:03.880 --> 00:37:05.540
you're giving it start and stop.

00:37:06.040 --> 00:37:08.220
So the first being start the second being stop

00:37:08.740 --> 00:37:12.020
and step gets this value of one by default.

00:37:12.680 --> 00:37:14.740
And if you give it three things in the parentheses

00:37:14.740 --> 00:37:18.360
you're giving it start stop and step in that order.

00:37:22.090 --> 00:37:25.710
And you're always going to start at the start value

00:37:25.710 --> 00:37:29.890
and stop at or so you're going to start at the start value

00:37:29.890 --> 00:37:32.150
and you're going to go until stop minus one.

00:37:32.270 --> 00:37:34.050
So those are the sequences of numbers.

00:37:36.390 --> 00:37:38.130
So in this first code right here

00:37:38.710 --> 00:37:40.750
my sum is going to get the value zero

00:37:40.750 --> 00:37:42.030
and you're going to have a for loop.

00:37:43.790 --> 00:37:46.230
We're going to start from seven right

00:37:46.230 --> 00:37:47.610
because we're giving it two numbers

00:37:48.050 --> 00:37:49.170
and when you give it two numbers

00:37:49.170 --> 00:37:52.390
it represents start and stop with step being one.

00:37:53.330 --> 00:37:54.650
So you're starting at seven.

00:37:55.750 --> 00:37:58.170
If step is one the next value is eight.

00:37:59.850 --> 00:38:01.270
What's the value after that?

00:38:04.800 --> 00:38:09.420
If we're incrementing by one nine.

00:38:12.360 --> 00:38:15.040
And since we're going until stop minus one

00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:18.940
we're not actually going to pick up on 10.

00:38:20.900 --> 00:38:23.780
So this loop variable I the very first time

00:38:23.780 --> 00:38:25.880
through the loop is going to have the value seven.

00:38:28.770 --> 00:38:33.510
So my sum is going to be zero plus seven.

00:38:37.300 --> 00:38:39.220
That's everything that's inside the code block.

00:38:40.160 --> 00:38:44.140
The next time through the loop I gets the value eight.

00:38:45.460 --> 00:38:53.060
So inside the for loop my sum gets whatever

00:38:53.460 --> 00:38:56.420
the previous value was which was seven plus eight.

00:38:59.860 --> 00:39:02.420
The next time through the loop my sum

00:39:04.120 --> 00:39:07.400
gets the value seven plus eight plus nine.

00:39:08.060 --> 00:39:10.400
Obviously replacing that with the previous value.

00:39:10.660 --> 00:39:11.020
So 15.

00:39:12.820 --> 00:39:15.440
Since we're not going through 10 that's where we stop

00:39:15.870 --> 00:39:17.340
and we're going to print out my sum

00:39:17.340 --> 00:39:19.720
which is going to be the value of seven plus eight plus nine.

00:39:22.370 --> 00:39:22.610
Oh yeah.

00:39:24.810 --> 00:39:25.390
Oh okay yeah.

00:39:25.390 --> 00:39:29.260
Do they have to be integers?

00:39:32.380 --> 00:39:33.400
That's a great question.

00:39:33.580 --> 00:39:34.340
We can try that out.

00:39:34.720 --> 00:39:37.660
I'm not actually sure right off just off the top of my head.

00:39:38.340 --> 00:39:40.540
So you can go in spider and say

00:39:45.430 --> 00:39:46.970
let's say in this example here.

00:39:47.110 --> 00:39:48.230
In this example here.

00:39:51.830 --> 00:39:55.310
So we can say seven point one 10.3.

00:39:58.220 --> 00:39:59.740
Yeah so they have to be integers.

00:40:08.550 --> 00:40:10.690
Okay so that's that's that example

00:40:10.690 --> 00:40:12.670
and let's erase that.

00:40:13.230 --> 00:40:16.310
In this particular example we have start stop and step

00:40:16.310 --> 00:40:19.110
and here we're going every other value.

00:40:19.910 --> 00:40:21.230
So we're starting at five.

00:40:22.050 --> 00:40:23.810
Tell me what the next value is supposed to be.

00:40:25.030 --> 00:40:26.590
If we're taking every other one.

00:40:27.750 --> 00:40:33.970
Seven and then nine and then are we doing 11 or not?

00:40:35.010 --> 00:40:36.550
Excellent nice yeah.

00:40:37.430 --> 00:40:38.930
So we're going to the end minus one.

00:40:40.850 --> 00:40:43.950
Okay so it's possible that sometimes you write code

00:40:43.950 --> 00:40:46.270
where you might want to exit out of the loop early.

00:40:46.910 --> 00:40:49.190
You don't want to go through all of the sequences

00:40:49.190 --> 00:40:50.550
of your numbers.

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:52.570
Maybe there's a condition inside there

00:40:52.570 --> 00:40:54.670
where you just want to exit the loop early.

00:40:55.390 --> 00:40:57.610
Inside the Y loop maybe you want to exit the loop

00:40:57.610 --> 00:40:59.270
before the condition becomes false.

00:41:00.190 --> 00:41:01.950
So that's where the break statement comes in.

00:41:02.890 --> 00:41:04.990
So the break works like this.

00:41:05.770 --> 00:41:09.450
It's going to as soon as Python sees this break statement

00:41:10.550 --> 00:41:13.550
it's going to say okay I'm going to look

00:41:13.550 --> 00:41:16.170
at whatever loop I'm currently in.

00:41:17.690 --> 00:41:20.850
I'm not evaluating any expression after it

00:41:20.850 --> 00:41:22.290
that comes within my loop

00:41:23.290 --> 00:41:25.610
and I'm going to immediately exit the loop.

00:41:25.870 --> 00:41:28.210
So I'm going inside this while this while

00:41:28.690 --> 00:41:30.290
I'm evaluating this one expression

00:41:30.290 --> 00:41:31.830
and then I suddenly see a break.

00:41:33.190 --> 00:41:35.730
Expression B does not get evaluated

00:41:37.670 --> 00:41:40.190
and break is going to immediately exit

00:41:40.190 --> 00:41:42.230
out of the innermost loop that it's in.

00:41:42.990 --> 00:41:45.730
So this Y loop that has condition two

00:41:45.730 --> 00:41:47.230
that's the innermost loop

00:41:47.230 --> 00:41:49.510
that the break is found in.

00:41:50.470 --> 00:41:53.850
So we're going to exit out of this innermost loop here

00:41:54.530 --> 00:41:56.450
and we're evaluating expression C

00:41:56.770 --> 00:41:58.810
and notice we're evaluating expression C

00:41:58.810 --> 00:42:03.150
because its expression C is part of the outer Y loop.

00:42:04.850 --> 00:42:07.630
It's at the same level as this one

00:42:08.130 --> 00:42:11.310
and these ones are part of the inner Y loop.

00:42:11.990 --> 00:42:15.290
Okay, last thing I want to say

00:42:15.290 --> 00:42:16.510
is just a little bit of a comparison

00:42:16.510 --> 00:42:17.810
between foreign Y loops.

00:42:18.270 --> 00:42:20.430
So when would you use one or the other?

00:42:20.950 --> 00:42:22.490
This might be useful in your problem sets.

00:42:23.070 --> 00:42:24.350
So for loops you usually use

00:42:24.350 --> 00:42:25.790
when you know the number of iterations.

00:42:26.330 --> 00:42:28.750
Okay, Y loops are very useful

00:42:28.750 --> 00:42:30.610
when for example you're getting user input

00:42:30.610 --> 00:42:32.610
and user input is unpredictable, right?

00:42:32.670 --> 00:42:33.750
You don't know how many times

00:42:33.750 --> 00:42:35.530
they're gonna do a certain task.

00:42:36.430 --> 00:42:37.850
For both foreign Y loops

00:42:37.850 --> 00:42:39.990
you can end out of the loop early using the break.

00:42:40.610 --> 00:42:41.950
The for loop uses this counter.

00:42:42.470 --> 00:42:43.950
It's inherent inside the for loop.

00:42:45.310 --> 00:42:47.290
A Y loop, you can use a counter

00:42:47.290 --> 00:42:49.710
in order you can use a Y loop to count things

00:42:50.490 --> 00:42:53.330
but you must initialize the counter before the Y loop

00:42:53.730 --> 00:42:55.930
and you have to remember to increment it within the loop

00:42:55.930 --> 00:42:58.070
otherwise you may be lead to an infinite loop.

00:43:00.720 --> 00:43:04.080
We've seen as the very first example of a for loop

00:43:04.080 --> 00:43:08.040
that the for loop could be rewritten as a Y loop

00:43:08.040 --> 00:43:09.940
but the vice versa is not necessarily true.

00:43:10.900 --> 00:43:14.100
And the counter example to that is just user input.

00:43:14.340 --> 00:43:15.980
So you might not know how many times

00:43:15.980 --> 00:43:17.420
you might do a certain task.

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:21.000
All right, great, that's all for today.

